"""The "Why it is here" paragraphs of the instructions of group mn-ext, which the prefix-coded encoding adds (tools/manual/isa_part4.py).  As in isa_stories.py:
written from the record only (the errata, the proposals and the measurements of the repository); where no reason is recorded the text says so."""

FUSED = ('The author decided the four fused pairs in errata 87 (`movcall`, `popmret`, `popret`, `mov2`) and left the fusing to the compiler: the assembler never fuses. '
         'The reason is the measurement of `docs/encoding-proposal.md` section 8 (static size of the training corpus, in the encoding): ')

STORY = {
'MOVCALL': FUSED + '`MOVQ Rs, Rd` followed by `CALL` occurs 11,123 times, and fusing it alone shrinks the code by 2.39%, the largest of the four; all four together fuse 20,428 pairs and save 3.18% of the bytes and about 1.1% of the executed instructions. The call keeps its reach (four or six bytes). The cost, named in the encoding study: a new mnemonic, and one instruction that writes two registers (the destination and the stack pointer), which is two micro-operations for an out-of-order core. The record gives no earlier machine for this particular pair; the reasoning is the fourth priority of the definitive list (errata 83), fewer instructions executed, where coalescing is good.',
'POPRET': FUSED + '`POP Rd` followed by `RET` and `POPM` followed by `RET` (`POPMRET`) occur 4,862 times together and save 1.00% of the bytes; this is the epilogue of nearly every function. The fault of the second half restarts the whole instruction, which is idempotent because the first half reads a register the second does not change and `POPM` is already specified restartable (decision 2 of the encoding study).',
'POPMRET': 'See `POPRET`: the two are measured together (4,862 pairs, 1.00% of the bytes). The mask is an 8-bit field scaled by 256 because one parcel has no room for a wider one; the epilogue that restores `R8` to `R15` is the common case the corpus shows, and any other set stays `POPM` and `RET`.',
'MOV2': FUSED + '`MOVQ Ra, Rb` followed by `MOVQ Rc, Rd` occurs 6,742 times and saves 0.50%, the smallest gain of the four and the dearest in code space: four 3-bit register fields use one sixteenth of the first-parcel space and can name only the eight hot registers. The encoding study lists both as costs, and one instruction that writes two registers (the rename width of an out-of-order core).',
'MASK': 'The author adopted the branch-free select (errata 85) when the crypto study asked for it: constant-time code must choose between two values without a branch, and a flag-reading conditional move would put a secret in a flag. `MASKEQZ` and `MASKNEZ` are the select of LoongArch and of RISC-V Zicond (stated from memory in the study, not checked against a manual); the pair and an `OR` make `c ? a : b` in three instructions, against two for `CMP` and a conditional move, but the conditional move would be used at 783 sites in 284,641 instructions (0.28%), and the case for the pair is the constant-time idiom, where the condition is already a register. The author confirmed the pair and wrote "looks good" on the section (errata 86). About 100 look-up tables, one cycle (the study\'s estimate).',
'BSEL': 'A bit select takes three sources and a destination, the four register fields of the three-operand group. Its case in the crypto proposal is SHA-2 (`Ch` and `Maj` are one `BSEL` each) and the constant-time swap: the X25519 ladder swaps ten limbs per step, 50 instructions with the mask pair against about 25 with `BSEL`. It is the select of ARM (`BSL`) and of x86 ternary logic, and it serves MD5 and SHA-1 round functions, blends and bit insertion. It is one of the select instructions of the secure core.',
'MOVQL': 'The author accepted it in the second round of security answers (errata 85, proposal P18). A 64-bit constant otherwise comes from the literal pool; the literal inside the instruction is one instruction, and it is not counted against the three-parcel rule because it is data, not instruction. The encoding work found no use of a trailing literal worth its exception to the fetch rule, so the literal is part of a five-parcel instruction (`docs/encoding-proposal.md` section 3).',
'OVF': 'Checked arithmetic is what clang `-ftrapv` and `__builtin_*_overflow`, Rust\'s debug overflow checks, Swift\'s default arithmetic and Ada\'s `Constraint_Error` need. The author decided `TRAPCC` with the fused `ADDV`, `SUBV` and `MULV` and no compare-and-trap form (errata 85), the fused forms as the case where one non-destructive instruction beats `ADD` and `TRAPCC`. The corpus cannot size the use: it contains no `BVS` or `BVC` at all, because the overflow builtins were not implemented in the compiler that produced it, and the study says that sanitiser builds are the target. The record gives the precedent only for the trap, not for the fused forms.',
'TRAPCC': 'A check that costs two bytes and no branch. The comparison with the alternatives is in the study (`docs/study-proposals.md` item 9): `ADD #1, 8(R2)` and a check is four plus two bytes and the add keeps its immediate and memory forms, where `ADD` and a `BVS` to a shared stub is six bytes and two instructions; and the same instruction checks what is not overflow (a bounds check after `CMP`, unsigned overflow after `ADD`). The OpenBSD kernels of i386, hppa and sparc64 already deliver integer-overflow traps as `SIGFPE` (`FPE_INTOVF`), so the signal route exists. The author made it a trap and not an abort (errata 85 and 86), so a handler that promotes a fixnum to a bignum can repair the register and resume; the one case where a trap defines `EVA`.',
'MOVD': 'The poison bit of item 10 of the study (`docs/study-proposals.md`) wants a load that can be hoisted above its guard: it either gives a value or a poisoned value, and a use of a poisoned value is the fault the guard would have prevented. That removes the recovery code of if-conversion and software pipelining, gives a deterministic Spectre version 1 form (a deferred, bounds-checked address computation yields poison, not a branch the predictor can train), and makes a chain of checks one fault. The author accepted the three leanings of the proposal (errata 86).',
'MOVUSER': 'Copying between the kernel and the user is where kernels pay for the user-memory guard. x86 opens a window with `STAC` and closes it with `CLAC` (35 and 53 sites in the kernel-assembly corpus), and a forgotten `CLAC` on an error path is the bug class; Arm\'s `LDTR` and `STTR` make the access itself unprivileged (52 sites), but only for plain loads and stores. Item 18 of the study makes user access an attribute of one operand, so that no window exists to leave open and the range check of the copy routines (`addr + len` against the top of the user half) disappears into the hardware: a kernel address faults at the first byte. The author accepted its five leanings (errata 86) and kept `PSW.UA`, `STUA` and `CLUA` until the OpenBSD port confirms.',
'MOVUSERD': 'See `MOVD` and `MOVUSER`. The record gives no separate reason for the combination; it is listed because both qualifiers exist and a probe of a user pointer wants both.',
'AESR': 'Constant-time software AES takes about 1,000 instructions a block (BearSSL\'s bitsliced code, scaled; the proposal\'s estimate), and table AES leaks through the cache. The author asked for constant-time AES and SHA instructions "or whichever opcodes would speed up and enable aes and sha" for the secure core (errata 85). The design is RISC-V\'s scalar cryptography (`aes64es` and its kin): the state is in the general registers, so no vector register file is needed and, unlike the AES drivers of OpenBSD for x86 and ARM, no `fpu_kernel_enter()` bracket either: a kernel AES loop saves nothing and is preemptible with the ordinary register save. A block costs 42 instructions for AES-128, 20 to 25 times fewer than the software sequence (estimates). Precedent for the pair-in-registers shape: RISC-V; the other machines (x86 AES-NI, ARM `AESE`) use vector registers.',
'AESK': 'The key schedule and the inverse mix of the round key complete the round instructions: three instructions per round of the AES-128 schedule, about 30 for the whole schedule instead of about 150, once per key; `AESIM` adds 18 for the decryption schedule. The same precedent as `AESR`.',
'SHA256': 'The SHA-2 rounds are rotations and three-input exclusive ors, which the integer instructions do in several steps; the RISC-V scalar cryptography extension has the eight functions as instructions. The crypto proposal counts the gain honestly: the primitives remove half the work, not nineteen twentieths, because a round still needs the adds and the register traffic (about 1,200 against 2,500 instructions for a 64-byte block with `BSEL`, estimates).',
'SHA512': 'See `SHA256`: the four 64-bit functions of SHA-512, with the same gain of about two (about 1,500 against 3,100 instructions for a 128-byte block, estimates).',
'MSHA': 'Stage 2 of the crypto proposal, in the manner of the s390 CPACF instructions (`KIMD` and kin): one instruction hashes a buffer, the padding and chaining are done in hardware and so cannot be got wrong, and progress is held in registers, so a fault or an interrupt restarts it, as for `MCPY`. The estimate is 64 cycles for a 64-byte SHA-256 block against about 1,200 instructions, roughly 18 times; the cost is gates in proportion to the algorithms frozen into opcodes. The proposal says to build `MSHA256` first and the others only after measurement, and the author has not decided it.',
'MAESCTR': 'Stage 2, as `MSHA`; whole-buffer AES gains less (about 4 times: 0.6 against 2.6 instructions per byte), because the round instructions are already good, and the proposal puts it after `MSHA256` and `MSHA512`.',
'SYSPG': 'The grammars reserve six words next to `LPAD` and `TRAPCC` for the page-bit mechanisms: the author approved landing pads gated by a page bit and the `SYS` page bit "to be specified" (errata 84), and the retire-stage study found that these need no opcode. The placeholders keep the space; the record does not say what any of them will be.',
}
